Same convention as the stage-1/1b record: ACCEPT = the implemented behaviour becomes the declaration; CHANGE = goes to the next engine pass (stage 4, after stage 3 lands). Nothing here is conditioned on a successor result; none exists.

1. **Formal provider identity** — DEFERRED-TO-SEAL: the provider is being implemented now in `/home/sat/mcrl-v025-codex-ws-provider` (`src/mcrl/physics_v025/provider_legacy.py`, `LegacyWorldProvider`), built on the legacy `ScenarioDriver`/TLE archive (`~/demo/tle_data/starlink/tle`, 373 daily files) with the repository world-seed rule and the TRAIN date split only. Its source SHA-256, TLE archive digest, user count (100), start-time derivation and cross-gain construction are sealed in the launch package after its KATs pass.
2. **v1.2 matrix arithmetic** — RESOLVED by the sealed v1.3 erratum (sha 62cecfc4…): 25 primary-eligible + 6 diagnostic = 31 settings; the explicit list and order govern; the launcher may reject the number 28.
3. **World duration / anchors** — **CHANGE**: a unit is not three isolated instants. Each fixed carrier (nearest-eligible, stay-if-possible, random-masked) is rolled for the 30 canonical steps and **every step is a decision instant** (30 anchors per carrier, 90 per world, as in the earlier oracle probe), each with its three-offset forecast steps. If the server-provider rehearsal projects more than 160 core-hours, thin by taking every k-th step with the smallest integer k that brings the projection under budget, chosen from the projection before any unit outcome is opened, and record k in the seal.
4. **Catalogue census** — **CHANGE** for real worlds: the complete Cartesian product of legal assignments is used only when it has ≤ 4 096 configurations (synthetic worlds). Otherwise the union catalogue is the bounded set: (i) every unilateral move of every user to every legal option; (ii) the S0 deployable proposals (top-two per user) and all frozen beam evacuations; (iii) all pairwise joint moves among the top-K = 10 users ranked by nominal unilateral surplus, over all their legal option pairs; (iv) per-active-beam evacuation sets (every user of the beam to its best legal alternative). J1 = the best changed-user-count > 1 configuration in this catalogue; U1 = the best in (i). The catalogue definition and its digest are sealed; the per-world superset check of stage-3 item 10 reports how many legal options fall outside the shortlist.
5. **LC-SRS e_i binding** — **CHANGE**: under the sealed whole-network C1 (which already contains every unilateral bit and joule change of the network), the unilateral externality e_i is inside C1, so the successor C3 target carries **no e_i term**: z₃,ᵢ = Ψ/2 for pairs and the equal-share (Shapley) split of the set interaction for larger sets, with Ψ computed on F = B − η_ref·E per regime (per-cell re-optimisation). This is the round-6 no-double-counting rule; the historical LC-SRS e_i (defined for an own-bits-only C1) is not transferred.
6. **QoS guard margins** — **CHANGE** (numeric, prospective): FULL is QoS non-inferior to a DROP arm when the 95 % cluster-bootstrap interval of (FULL − DROP) lies above −0.5 pp for complete-service availability, and the interval of the relative change lies below +5 % for both the handover rate and the Φ-priced handover cost per user-step. Catalogue/service exclusion thresholds do not exclude configurations; they score and report (the service guard used by the deployable decoder is the sealed one from C3-S: no served-count decrease versus the BASE proposal).
7. **Nominal-greedy lexicographic rule** — ACCEPT (served users, then bits, then lower energy, then configuration id; no price).
