# Beamwidth source audit (Task 1 only)

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM` — no constant or sealed/engine path was changed.

## Finding

**HOBS uses `theta_3dB = 0.058 rad` as the one-sided angle from boresight to
the half-power point.** It is not the full angular span between the two
half-power points. The source-implied full HPBW is therefore `0.116 rad =
6.6463104235 deg`.

Completion criterion: verify the cited paper's equation and Table I row in a
retrievable primary-source copy, resolve the angle convention from the equation,
and trace convention-coupled constants and downstream geometry in the engine.
This criterion is met.

## Primary source and custody

Sz-Han Chen, Li-Hsiang Shen, Kai-Ten Feng, Lie-Liang Yang, and Jen-Ming Wu,
“Energy-Efficient Joint Handover and Beam Switching Scheme for Multi-LEO
Networks,” *2024 IEEE 99th Vehicular Technology Conference
(VTC2024-Spring)*, DOI
[10.1109/VTC2024-Spring62846.2024.10683088](https://doi.org/10.1109/VTC2024-Spring62846.2024.10683088).

- [Official VTC conference PDF](https://ieeevtc.org/vtc2024spring/DATA/PID2024002205.pdf)
- [IEEE Xplore record](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10683088)
- Audited local IEEE PDF:
  [reference PDF](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/chinese-word-r1-symbols-20260905-r1/input/thesis-mc/references-pdf/%5B04%5D_Energy_Efficient_Joint_Handover_and_Beam_Switching_S.pdf),
  7 pages, SHA-256
  `1b2a8eda4647c4f7b68f8ffb08347aaa9cde18ae32b7d6af84d68f827a0f3089`.
  This digest matches the repository's prior custody record in
  [`ADR-002`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/docs/decisions/ADR-002-proposed-hobs-primary-geometry.md:20).

## Verbatim source evidence

Equation (3), PDF page 2:

> `G(theta) = G_0 [ J_1(mu(theta))/(2 mu(theta)) + 36 J_3(mu(theta))/mu(theta)^3 ]^2`

The same paragraph defines:

> `mu(theta) = 2.07123 * sin(theta)/sin(theta_3dB)`

Its short prose definitions are “where theta is the boresight angle” and
“theta_3dB is the 3 dB half-power beamwidth angle of the antenna.”

Table I, PDF page 6, verbatim row:

> `3dB beamwidth theta_3dB | 0.058 rad`

Symbols have been rendered in ASCII above, without changing the source's
operators, subscripts, coefficient, or value.

## Why the equation settles the convention

At `theta = theta_3dB`, the source's denominator cancels its numerator and
`mu = 2.07123`. Direct high-precision evaluation of equation (3) gives

```text
G(theta_3dB) / G_0 = 0.5000004083327867
10 log10(G/G_0)     = -3.0102964099 dB
```

At `theta = 0`, the Bessel-expression limit is one, so `G(0) = G_0`; the
pattern is symmetric about boresight. Thus the parameter used as the angular
argument in the equation is the radial/off-boresight location of the
half-power boundary. The full width of a one-dimensional cut is the span from
`-theta_3dB` to `+theta_3dB`, namely twice the table value:

```text
source half-power off-axis angle = 0.058 rad = 3.3231552118 deg
source-implied full HPBW          = 0.116 rad = 6.6463104235 deg
```

HOBS does not itself print `0.116 rad`; that full-span value is the necessary
consequence of its equation. The table heading alone is linguistically
ambiguous, but the equation is not: it contains `sin(theta_3dB)`, not
`sin(theta_3dB/2)`.

## Engine reading and propagation audit

| Item | What the code/source does | Convention impact |
|---|---|---|
| `TX_FULL_HPBW_DEG = 3.32` | Declared as a full HPBW in [`constants_v025.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/constants_v025.py:76). [`channel.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/channel.py:99) divides it by two before putting it in the denominator of `mu`. | The V0.25 pattern's half-power edge is `1.66 deg`, half the HOBS off-axis value. At the engine's `theta = 3.32 deg`, its normalized gain is only `0.0423687169` (`-13.72955 dB`), not one half. |
| `_BESSEL_MU_COEFFICIENT = 2.07123` | Copied from HOBS equation (3) in [`channel.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/channel.py:39). | Correct and invariant; the convention error is the extra halving of the angle supplied to its denominator. |
| `TX_G0_LINEAR = 2000` | Not HOBS Table I's `40 dBi` (`10000`). The legacy antenna record says it was re-derived from `D = 1.0275 lambda / theta_3dB` using the narrower full-width convention and an external aperture-efficiency range; see [`env/antenna.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/env/antenna.py:46). | This is convention-coupled provenance. Under that same project aperture/width relation, doubling full HPBW halves `D/lambda` and quarters gain, giving about `500`; the repository correctly labels that as an unresolved conditional candidate, not a HOBS value ([`ADR-002`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/docs/decisions/ADR-002-proposed-hobs-primary-geometry.md:140)). A width-only diagnostic that holds `2000` fixed is therefore a deliberate sensitivity, not a fully re-derived HOBS geometry. |
| Legacy `env.THETA_3DB_DEG = 3.32` | Duplicates the full-span reading in both [`env/antenna.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/env/antenna.py:43) and [`env/cells.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/env/cells.py:49). `cell_radius_km` also halves it. | The same interpretation propagates beyond link gain into cell radius, lattice pitch, coverage, and any frozen pointing-cell selection built from that grid. [`prereg.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/runtime/prereg.py:626) records that geometry explicitly. |
| `REFERENCE_BEAMS_PER_SATELLITE = 39` and derived `REFERENCE_AGGREGATE_RF_W` | V0.25 calls `39` a compatibility reference rather than a hardware ceiling in [`constants_v025.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/constants_v025.py:88). HOBS Table I says `37`, so `39` is not copied from HOBS. | Not an arithmetic dependent of `TX_FULL_HPBW_DEG` in V0.25, but its project lineage is the narrow-grid coverage selection, so a geometry rebuild must not assume its provenance survives a width change. |
| `HOBS_REFERENCE_MAX_RF_W = 100` | Literal conversion of HOBS Table I's `P_max = 50 dBm`, retained only as a non-live compatibility reference in [`constants_v025.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/constants_v025.py:90). | Same paper, but independent of the angle convention; no propagation. |

No other constant in `src/mcrl/physics_v025` arithmetically depends on
`TX_FULL_HPBW_DEG`. The actual V0.25 dependence is confined to the transmit
pattern calculation; the separate legacy `env` geometry carries the broader
cell/lattice dependence described above.

One additional provenance caution: [`env/antenna.py`](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/env/antenna.py:65)
says the paper fixes an on-axis cutoff of `1e-10`. A full-text search of the
audited HOBS PDF finds no `1e-10` cutoff; HOBS states the analytic pattern but
does not state that numerical implementation tolerance. This does not affect
the beamwidth conclusion, but it should not be attributed to HOBS without a
different cited source.

## Task-bound conclusion

The reported audit reading is correct: HOBS's `0.058 rad` is the half-power
off-axis angle, implying a full span twice as wide as the engine's registered
`3.32 deg` full HPBW. This is a source-definition finding only. It neither
changes a constant nor recommends selecting a width from outcome performance.
"""Dense catalogue evaluation for the V0.25 rate-target TDM architecture.

This module is deliberately narrow: it implements the exact ``a-r`` radiation
and integration equations for a whole configuration catalogue backed by a
``PrimitiveStepArrays`` object.  The scalar object path remains the authority
used by KATs; this path removes per-row Python objects from real-world units.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass
import math

import numpy as np

from mcrl.errors import MCRLContractError

from .acm import ACM_MODES, rate_target_mode, rate_target_sinr
from .channel import fading_product_quantile_array, noise_power_w
from .constants_v025 import (
    BASEBAND_POWER_PER_ACTIVE_SATELLITE_W,
    BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ,
    BEAM_RF_CAP_W,
    CIRCUIT_POWER_PER_CHAIN_W,
    D2_MEASUREMENT_STEP_S,
    PA_MAX_EFFICIENCY,
    PA_SATURATION_POWER_W,
    POWER_SOLVER_ITERATION_CAP,
    POWER_SOLVER_TOLERANCE_W,
    RATE_TARGET_BPS,
    SINR_MIN_DB,
)
from .tapes import PrimitiveStepArrays


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class BatchARResult:
    bits: np.ndarray
    joules: np.ndarray
    pa_j: np.ndarray
    circuit_j: np.ndarray
    baseband_j: np.ndarray
    decoding_time_s: np.ndarray
    feasible: np.ndarray
    attained: np.ndarray
    residual_w: np.ndarray
    mode_counts: np.ndarray
    target_mode_counts: np.ndarray
    transmitted_mode_counts: np.ndarray
    realised_decode_successes: np.ndarray
    realised_decode_failures: np.ndarray
    plateau_users: np.ndarray
    transmissions: np.ndarray
    cap_hits: np.ndarray
    valid: np.ndarray
    certificate_status: np.ndarray
    certificate_iterations: np.ndarray
    max_rf_power_w: np.ndarray
    min_decoding_margin_db: np.ndarray
    mean_acm_se_bit_s_hz: np.ndarray


def _readonly(value: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
    result = np.asarray(value)
    result.setflags(write=False)
    return result


def _slot_grid(maximum_occupancy: int) -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:
    boundaries = {0.0, 1.0}
    for occupancy in range(1, maximum_occupancy + 1):
        boundaries.update(index / occupancy for index in range(1, occupancy))
    ordered = np.asarray(sorted(boundaries), dtype=np.float64)
    return (ordered[1:] - ordered[:-1], (ordered[1:] + ordered[:-1]) / 2.0)


def _evaluate_ar_tdm_catalogue_core(
    arrays: PrimitiveStepArrays,
    selected_rows: np.ndarray,
    *,
    field: str = "realised",
    chunk_size: int = 256,
    rate_target_bps: float = RATE_TARGET_BPS,
    circuit_power_per_active_chain_w: float = CIRCUIT_POWER_PER_CHAIN_W,
    boundary_indices: tuple[int, ...] = tuple(range(48)),
    fading_quantile_alpha: float | None = None,
) -> BatchARResult:
    """Evaluate ``C x U`` selected candidate-row indices without row objects.

    A row index of ``-1`` is the explicit null action.  The common rational
    TDM partition is an exact refinement of every configuration's scalar slot
    partition, so splitting a constant slot cannot alter rates or energy.
    """

    rows_all = np.asarray(selected_rows, dtype=np.int64)
    if rows_all.ndim != 2 or rows_all.shape[1] != arrays.users.size:
        raise MCRLContractError("selected_rows must have shape (configurations, users)")
    if field not in {"nominal", "margin", "realised"}:
        raise MCRLContractError("batch field must be nominal, margin, or realised")
    if fading_quantile_alpha is not None and (
        not math.isfinite(fading_quantile_alpha)
        or not 0.0 < fading_quantile_alpha < 1.0
    ):
        raise MCRLContractError("fading quantile alpha must lie strictly in (0,1)")
    if field == "margin" and fading_quantile_alpha is None:
        raise MCRLContractError("margin field requires a fading quantile alpha")
    if (
        not boundary_indices
        or tuple(sorted(set(boundary_indices))) != boundary_indices
        or boundary_indices[0] < 0
        or boundary_indices[-1] >= 48
    ):
        raise MCRLContractError("boundary indices must be unique, ordered, and in 0..47")
    configurations, users = rows_all.shape
    modes = ACM_MODES
    thresholds = np.asarray([row.threshold_linear for row in modes])
    efficiencies = np.asarray([row.spectral_efficiency_bit_per_s_hz for row in modes])
    gamma = np.asarray(
        [0.0]
        + [
            np.nan if rate_target_sinr(rate_target_bps, BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ, n) is None
            else float(rate_target_sinr(rate_target_bps, BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ, n))
            for n in range(1, users + 1)
        ],
        dtype=np.float64,
    )
    target_mode_index = np.asarray(
        [0]
        + [
            0
            if rate_target_mode(rate_target_bps, BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ, n) is None
            else ACM_MODES.index(
                rate_target_mode(rate_target_bps, BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ, n)
            )
            + 1
            for n in range(1, users + 1)
        ],
        dtype=np.int64,
    )
    noise = noise_power_w(BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ)
    bits = np.zeros((configurations, users), dtype=np.float64)
    decoding = np.zeros_like(bits)
    feasible_all = np.ones((configurations, users), dtype=np.bool_)
    residual_max = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.float64)
    components = np.zeros((configurations, 3), dtype=np.float64)
    mode_counts = np.zeros((configurations, len(modes) + 1), dtype=np.int64)
    target_mode_counts = np.zeros_like(mode_counts)
    transmitted_mode_counts = np.zeros_like(mode_counts)
    realised_decode_successes = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    realised_decode_failures = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    plateau = np.ones((configurations, users), dtype=np.bool_)
    seen = np.zeros((configurations, users), dtype=np.bool_)
    transmissions = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    cap_hits = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    valid_result = np.ones(configurations, dtype=np.bool_)
    # 0=INVALID, 1=CONVERGED, 2=CONVERGED_SLOW.  A configuration receives
    # the worst certificate observed over its slots and selected boundaries.
    certificate_status = np.ones(configurations, dtype=np.int8)
    certificate_iterations = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    max_rf_power_w = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.float64)
    min_decoding_margin_db = np.full(configurations, np.inf, dtype=np.float64)
    acm_se_sum = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.float64)
    acm_se_count = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    row_to_aggressor = {
        (int(identity[0]), int(identity[1])): index
        for index, identity in enumerate(arrays.aggressor_identities)
    }
    row_aggressor = np.asarray(
        [
            row_to_aggressor.get((int(identity[0]), int(identity[1])), -1)
            for identity in arrays.identities
        ],
        dtype=np.int64,
    )
    beam_code_of = {
        identity: index
        for index, identity in enumerate(
            sorted({(int(row[0]), int(row[1])) for row in arrays.identities.tolist()})
        )
    }
    row_beam_code = np.asarray(
        [
            beam_code_of[(int(identity[0]), int(identity[1]))]
            for identity in arrays.identities
        ],
        dtype=np.int64,
    )
    # Keep rare high-occupancy evacuation profiles out of ordinary chunks;
    # otherwise one such row forces every neighbouring unilateral through a
    # much finer universal rational slot grid.
    initial_maximum = np.zeros(configurations, dtype=np.int64)
    for config_index, selected in enumerate(rows_all):
        selected_codes = row_beam_code[selected[selected >= 0]]
        if selected_codes.size:
            initial_maximum[config_index] = int(
                np.max(np.unique(selected_codes, return_counts=True)[1])
            )
    order = np.argsort(initial_maximum, kind="stable")
    inverse_order = np.argsort(order)
    rows_all = rows_all[order]
    satellite_of = {
        int(identity[0]): int(column)
        for identity, column in zip(
            arrays.aggressor_identities,
            arrays.aggressor_satellite_column,
            strict=True,
        )
    }
    row_satellite = np.asarray(
        [satellite_of.get(int(identity[0]), 0) for identity in arrays.identities],
        dtype=np.int64,
    )
    user_axis = np.arange(users, dtype=np.int64)
    lower_user = user_axis[None, None, :] < user_axis[None, :, None]
    # Quantiles belong to immutable candidate rows, not configurations.  Build
    # each boundary's row table once and gather from it in every catalogue
    # chunk; recomputing unique bins over C x U selections dominated the real
    # four-worker gate despite every scalar quantile already being cached.
    quantile_by_boundary = (
        {}
        if fading_quantile_alpha is None
        else {
            boundary: fading_product_quantile_array(
                arrays.elevations_deg[boundary], fading_quantile_alpha
            )
            for boundary in boundary_indices
        }
    )

    for low in range(0, configurations, chunk_size):
        high = min(configurations, low + chunk_size)
        selected = rows_all[low:high]
        count = high - low
        valid_assignment = selected >= 0
        safe_rows = np.maximum(selected, 0)
        aggressor = row_aggressor[safe_rows]
        safe_aggressor = np.maximum(aggressor, 0)
        beam_code = row_beam_code[safe_rows]
        victim_color = arrays.colors[safe_rows]
        wanted_slot = arrays.row_wanted_slot[safe_rows]
        aggressor_color = victim_color
        aggressor_satellite = row_satellite[safe_rows]
        aggressor_norad = arrays.identities[safe_rows, 0]
        previous_rate = previous_decode = previous_energy = None

        previous_boundary = None
        for boundary in boundary_indices:
            live = valid_assignment & arrays.visible[boundary, safe_rows] \
                & arrays.d2_eligible[boundary, safe_rows] \
                & arrays.cell_reachable[boundary, safe_rows]
            same_beam = beam_code[:, :, None] == beam_code[:, None, :]
            occupancy = np.sum(same_beam & live[:, None, :], axis=2)
            maximum = int(np.max(occupancy, initial=1))
            fractions, midpoints = _slot_grid(maximum)
            boundary_rate = np.zeros((count, users), dtype=np.float64)
            boundary_decode = np.zeros((count, users), dtype=np.bool_)
            boundary_feasible = np.zeros((count, users), dtype=np.bool_)
            boundary_energy = np.zeros((count, 3), dtype=np.float64)
            rank = np.sum(same_beam & live[:, None, :] & lower_user, axis=2)
            direct_nominal = arrays.nominal_gain[boundary, safe_rows]
"""Five V0.25 radiation architectures behind one immutable interface."""

from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass
import math
from typing import Literal, Protocol

import numpy as np

from mcrl.errors import MCRLContractError

from .acm import (
    ACMMode,
    ACMRate,
    ACMRealisedOutcome,
    decode_transmitted_mode,
    rate_target_mode,
    rate_target_sinr,
    select_transmitted_mode,
)
from .channel import fading_product_quantile, noise_power_w
from .constants_v025 import (
    BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ,
    BEAM_RF_CAP_W,
    POWER_CONTROL_TARGET_LINEAR,
    RATE_TARGET_BPS,
    POWER_SOLVER_ITERATION_CAP,
    POWER_SOLVER_TOLERANCE_W,
)

BeamIdentity = tuple[int, int]
FieldKind = Literal["nominal", "margin", "realised"]
ArchitectureCode = Literal["b", "a-\u03b3", "a\u2032-\u03b3", "a-r", "a\u2032-r"]


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Link:
    """One attempted user link and its physical beam identity."""

    user_id: int
    beam: BeamIdentity
    color: int
    nominal_gain: float
    realised_gain: float | None = None
    elevation_deg: float = 90.0

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        if type(self.user_id) is not int or self.user_id < 0:
            raise MCRLContractError("user_id must be a nonnegative exact integer")
        if len(self.beam) != 2 or any(type(value) is not int for value in self.beam):
            raise MCRLContractError("beam must be the physical (NORAD, chain) identity")
        if type(self.color) is not int or self.color < 0:
            raise MCRLContractError("color must be a nonnegative exact integer")
        for label, value in (
            ("nominal_gain", self.nominal_gain),
            ("realised_gain", self.realised_gain),
        ):
            if value is not None and (not math.isfinite(value) or value <= 0.0):
                raise MCRLContractError(f"{label} must be finite and positive")
        if not math.isfinite(self.elevation_deg) or not -90.0 <= self.elevation_deg <= 90.0:
            raise MCRLContractError("elevation_deg must be finite and in [-90,90]")


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Geometry:
    """A history-free scheduled geometry sample.

    Cross-gain entry ``[victim, aggressor]`` is received watts per aggressor
    RF watt before colour, serving-beam, and waveform-overlap masks.
    """

    links: tuple[Link, ...]
    nominal_cross_gain: np.ndarray
    realised_cross_gain: np.ndarray | None = None

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        users = len(self.links)
        if users == 0:
            if np.asarray(self.nominal_cross_gain).shape != (0, 0):
                raise MCRLContractError("empty geometry needs a (0,0) cross-gain matrix")
            empty = np.zeros((0, 0), dtype=np.float64)
            empty.setflags(write=False)
            object.__setattr__(self, "nominal_cross_gain", empty)
            object.__setattr__(self, "realised_cross_gain", None)
            return
        ids = [link.user_id for link in self.links]
        if len(set(ids)) != users:
            raise MCRLContractError("user identities must be unique")
        for name in ("nominal_cross_gain", "realised_cross_gain"):
            value = getattr(self, name)
            if value is None:
                continue
            matrix = np.asarray(value, dtype=np.float64)
            if matrix.shape != (users, users):
                raise MCRLContractError(f"{name} must have shape ({users},{users})")
            if not np.all(np.isfinite(matrix)) or np.any(matrix < 0.0):
                raise MCRLContractError(f"{name} must be finite and nonnegative")
            frozen = np.array(matrix, copy=True)
            frozen.setflags(write=False)
            object.__setattr__(self, name, frozen)

    def gains(
        self, field: FieldKind, *, fading_quantile_alpha: float | None = None
    ) -> np.ndarray:
        if field == "nominal":
            return np.asarray([link.nominal_gain for link in self.links], dtype=np.float64)
        if field == "margin":
            if fading_quantile_alpha is None:
                raise MCRLContractError("margin field requires a fading quantile alpha")
            return np.asarray(
                [
                    link.nominal_gain
                    * fading_product_quantile(
                        link.elevation_deg, fading_quantile_alpha
                    )
                    for link in self.links
                ],
                dtype=np.float64,
            )
        return np.asarray(
            [
                link.nominal_gain if link.realised_gain is None else link.realised_gain
                for link in self.links
            ],
            dtype=np.float64,
        )

    def cross(self, field: FieldKind) -> np.ndarray:
        if field == "margin":
            return np.asarray(self.nominal_cross_gain, dtype=np.float64)
        if field == "realised" and self.realised_cross_gain is not None:
            return np.asarray(self.realised_cross_gain, dtype=np.float64)
        return np.asarray(self.nominal_cross_gain, dtype=np.float64)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class RadiationConfig:
    bandwidth_hz: float = BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ
    beam_cap_w: float = BEAM_RF_CAP_W
    target_sinr: float = POWER_CONTROL_TARGET_LINEAR
    rate_target_bps: float = RATE_TARGET_BPS
    solver_tolerance_w: float = POWER_SOLVER_TOLERANCE_W
    solver_iteration_cap: int = POWER_SOLVER_ITERATION_CAP
    fading_quantile_alpha: float | None = None

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        scalars = (
            self.bandwidth_hz,
            self.beam_cap_w,
            self.target_sinr,
            self.rate_target_bps,
            self.solver_tolerance_w,
        )
        if any(not math.isfinite(value) or value <= 0.0 for value in scalars):
            raise MCRLContractError("radiation configuration values must be positive")
        if self.fading_quantile_alpha is not None and (
            not math.isfinite(self.fading_quantile_alpha)
            or not 0.0 < self.fading_quantile_alpha < 1.0
        ):
            raise MCRLContractError("fading quantile alpha must lie strictly in (0,1)")
        if type(self.solver_iteration_cap) is not int or self.solver_iteration_cap < 1:
            raise MCRLContractError("solver_iteration_cap must be a positive integer")


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class PowerCertificate:
    status: Literal["FIXED", "CONVERGED", "CONVERGED_SLOW", "INVALID"]
    iterations: int
    residual_w: float
    tolerance_w: float
    saturated_users: tuple[int, ...] = ()

    @property
    def valid(self) -> bool:
        return self.status != "INVALID"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Transmission:
    user_id: int
    beam: BeamIdentity
    rf_power_w: float
    wanted_w: float
    intra_interference_w: float
    inter_interference_w: float
    noise_w: float
    bandwidth_hz: float
    subband: tuple[float, float]
    rate_target_bps: float | None = None
    rate_target_sinr: float | None = None
    rate_target_feasible: bool | None = None
    m_target: ACMMode | None = None
    m_tx: ACMMode | None = None
    realised_outcome: ACMRealisedOutcome | None = None
    nominal_sinr: float | None = None
    margin_predicted_sinr: float | None = None

    @property
    def interference_w(self) -> float:
        return self.intra_interference_w + self.inter_interference_w

    @property
    def sinr(self) -> float:
        return self.wanted_w / (self.interference_w + self.noise_w)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class RadiationSlot:
    start_fraction: float
    end_fraction: float
    beam_rf_w: tuple[tuple[BeamIdentity, float], ...]
    transmissions: tuple[Transmission, ...]
    certificate: PowerCertificate

    @property
    def fraction(self) -> float:
        return self.end_fraction - self.start_fraction


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class RadiationResult:
    architecture: ArchitectureCode
    field: FieldKind
    slots: tuple[RadiationSlot, ...]
    certificate: PowerCertificate
    rate_target_bps: float | None = None

    @property
    def valid(self) -> bool:
        return self.certificate.valid

    @property
    def per_user_wanted_w(self) -> dict[int, float]:
        result: dict[int, float] = {}
        for slot in self.slots:
            for tx in slot.transmissions:
                result[tx.user_id] = result.get(tx.user_id, 0.0) + slot.fraction * tx.wanted_w
        return result

    @property
    def per_user_interference_w(self) -> dict[int, tuple[float, float]]:
        result: dict[int, tuple[float, float]] = {}
        for slot in self.slots:
            for tx in slot.transmissions:
                old = result.get(tx.user_id, (0.0, 0.0))
                result[tx.user_id] = (
                    old[0] + slot.fraction * tx.intra_interference_w,
                    old[1] + slot.fraction * tx.inter_interference_w,
                )
        return result

    @property
    def per_user_rate_target_feasible(self) -> dict[int, bool] | None:
        """Controller feasibility by user, distinct from realised PHY service."""

        rows: dict[int, bool] = {}
        saw_target = False
        for slot in self.slots:
            for tx in slot.transmissions:
                if tx.rate_target_feasible is None:
                    continue
                saw_target = True
                rows[tx.user_id] = rows.get(tx.user_id, True) and tx.rate_target_feasible
        return rows if saw_target or self.rate_target_bps is not None else None

    @property
    def rate_target_attained(self) -> dict[int, bool] | None:
        """Realised achieved-ACM target status over this normalized user-step."""

        transmission_targets = {
            tx.rate_target_bps
            for slot in self.slots
            for tx in slot.transmissions
            if tx.rate_target_bps is not None
        }
        if self.rate_target_bps is None:
            if transmission_targets:
                raise MCRLContractError("non-target radiation contains a rate target")
            return None
        if transmission_targets and transmission_targets != {self.rate_target_bps}:
            raise MCRLContractError("radiation result contains inconsistent rate targets")
        target = self.rate_target_bps
        rates: dict[int, float] = {}
        for slot in self.slots:
            for tx in slot.transmissions:
                rates.setdefault(tx.user_id, 0.0)
                if tx.realised_outcome is None:
                    raise MCRLContractError("ACM transmission lacks a decode outcome")
                if tx.realised_outcome.decoded:
                    rates[tx.user_id] += (
                        slot.fraction
                        * tx.bandwidth_hz
                        * tx.realised_outcome.credited_spectral_efficiency_bit_per_s_hz
                    )
        return {user: rate >= target for user, rate in rates.items()}


class RadiationArchitecture(Protocol):
    code: ArchitectureCode

